SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) environment supporting multiple users, role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, revocation, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces authorization correctness, maintains delegation and permission consistency, and ensures safety and liveness under concurrency. Users have clearance levels (High, Medium, Low), and objects have classification levels (High, Medium, Low). Requests are classified by the highest classification level of affected objects and must comply with user clearance. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject lower-clearance user requests. Delegation and revocation of roles are governed by clearance and administrative rules. The system includes an Emergency Module to manage exceptional scenarios, which alters standard behaviors under specific triggers and constraints. Emergency mode is abstracted as a fixed duration but may terminate early if both administrators who activated it jointly deactivate it. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- The set of administrators can range from 0 to 4, depending on scenario. Administrator uniqueness is based on set semantics; duplicate entries are not possible.
- Emergency mode can only be triggered from Normal mode and requires exactly two distinct administrators, acting jointly (no more, no fewer).
- Emergency mode is time-bounded, abstracted as a fixed duration; it may terminate early if both participating administrators jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.
- FIFO ordering for request processing can be bypassed only during Emergency mode, and only for high-classification requests.
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users only during Emergency mode. During Emergency mode, it must be possible and observable for any user to simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including roles that are mutually exclusive in Normal mode.
- Emergency privilege is granted only to the two administrators who triggered Emergency mode (emAdmins), only during Emergency mode, and is revoked immediately and always at the conclusion of Emergency mode. EmergencyPrivilege must never be granted outside Emergency mode.
- Roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked when Emergency mode ends; specifically, any delegation assigned during Emergency mode must end at the first Normal state after Emergency ends. This is strictly mandatory.
- Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode are not automatically revoked, but may still expire or be revoked by an administrator during Emergency mode.
- Only the administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger (the two emAdmins) are required to update their credentials after Emergency mode ends.
- In Emergency module verification scenarios, at least one Emergency state, at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles, at least one user holding both roles of a mutually exclusive pair during Emergency mode, and at least one delegation present during Emergency mode must exist. Scenarios with no mutually exclusive roles or no delegation are permissible except when verifying Emergency mode behavior.
- No role is mutually exclusive with itself, and mutual exclusivity is symmetric.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Trigger  
Exactly two distinct administrators, while the system is in Normal mode, are required to jointly trigger entry into Emergency mode. No more and no fewer than two administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode. Only these two participating administrators (emAdmins) are considered for all subsequent emergency-related requirements.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a limited (bounded) time, abstracted as a fixed duration; however, it may terminate early if both participating administrators (emAdmins) jointly deactivate Emergency. The system must eventually return to Normal mode after Emergency mode.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
R3.1. During Emergency mode, request processing must occur by classification: all high-classification requests are processed first (possibly concurrently), then all medium-classification requests are processed concurrently, and finally all low-classification requests are processed.  
R3.2. During Emergency mode, mutual exclusivity of roles is suspended for all users. It must be possible and observable for any user to simultaneously hold any combination of roles, including at least one pair of roles that are mutually exclusive in Normal mode.  
    - Note: For verification purposes only, Emergency-mode scenarios must always include at least one Emergency state, at least one pair of mutually exclusive roles, at least one user holding both such roles during Emergency, and at least one delegation present during Emergency. This is a modeling constraint for coverage, not a system/business requirement. Also, verify that it is not possible for any user to hold mutually exclusive roles in Normal mode.
R3.3. During Emergency mode, only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger (emAdmins) temporarily hold emergency privileges, and EmergencyPrivilege is granted only to them for the duration of Emergency mode. EmergencyPrivilege must never be granted outside Emergency mode and must always be revoked immediately after Emergency mode ends.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1. After Emergency mode ends, emergency privileges must be revoked from the two administrators (emAdmins) who participated in the Emergency trigger.  
R4.2. After Emergency mode ends, any roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked; specifically:  
  - R4.2a. Any delegation assigned during Emergency mode must end at the first Normal state after Emergency ends. This is strictly mandatory.  
  - R4.2b. Delegated roles from before Emergency mode are not automatically revoked solely due to the transition out of Emergency mode; they remain subject to normal expiration or administrator revocation, including during Emergency mode.  
R4.3. After Emergency mode ends, only the two administrators who participated in the Emergency trigger are required to update their credentials.

R5: Delegation Clearance  
R5.1. Users with higher clearance may temporarily delegate one or more of their assigned roles to a lower-clearance user, provided that the delegate meets the role’s minimum clearance requirement.  
R5.2. Delegation can only occur if the delegator holds the role at the time of delegation, and the delegatee meets the clearance requirement at the time of delegation.  
R5.3. No delegation can expire before it is granted.

